            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Spiritual discernment and the God-given capacity to comprehend divine truth, which includes illumination of the Scriptures, knowledge of God's will, and the moral discernment to live rightly.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The moral discernment of good from evil, which is specifically gained by practical obedience and departing from evil.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Comprehension of divine truth and spiritual/moral discernment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Moral discernment of good and evil tied to obedience.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus includes a broader cognitive and spiritual dimension, such as the illumination of divine mysteries and comprehension of spiritual truths (prominent in the New Testament). The book restricts the definition to the moral and practical dimension of distinguishing good from evil through obedience (prominent in Wisdom literature).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book identifies a universally recognized facet of the consensus but treats it as the exhaustive definition, leaving out broader aspects of spiritual illumination.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By defining understanding exclusively in terms of departing from evil and keeping commandments, the book narrows the consensus's broader definition of spiritual comprehension to its purely moral and obedient dimension.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's definition is simply a direct quotation/summary of the specific verses it cites (Job 28:28, Ps 111:10), which the consensus also affirms; characterizing this as a 'narrowing' might overstate a mere difference in focus or brevity.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
